* {{l|en|TINSTAAFL}} (there is no such thing as a free lunch)

===Etymology===
Acronym, originating in the early 20th century, and popularized by Robert Heinlein's novel ''The Moon Is a Harsh Mistress''.

===Phrase===
{{en-phrase}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} [[there ain't no such thing as a free lunch|There ain't no such thing as a free lunch]]; something advertised as being "free" will invariably have hidden costs.
#* {{quote-text|year=1966|author={{w|Robert Heinlein}}|title={{w|The Moon Is A Harsh Mistress}}|passage="Gospodin," he said presently, "you used an odd word earlier--odd to me, I mean..."}}
#*: "Oh, ''''tanstaafl'''.' Means 'There ain't no such thing as a free lunch.' And isn't," I added, pointing to a FREE LUNCH sign across room, "or these drinks would cost half as much. Was reminding her that anything free costs twice as much in long run or turns out worthless."
#* {{quote-text|year=1985|author={{w|Lance Parkin}}|title=The Infinity Doctors|page=229|passage=''''Tanstaafl'''. There ain't no such thing as a free lunch. We both know that I was brought here for a reason. If we could get down to business.'}}

